{
  "families": [
    {
      "budget_share": 0.8,
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"}
      ],
      "why": "Iterative shares one datapath, so it gives the smallest LUT+FF for any given accuracy. Throughput is Fmax/(N+3), which for N≤20 and any realistic Artix-7 Fmax (≥100 MHz) is ≥4 MSPS, safely above the 1 MSPS constraint. Thus area is the only binding objective here, and iterative should dominate the Pareto front. The ranges cover the accuracy threshold (W≥12, N≥10) and extend high enough to reach the accuracy objective and the HV reference area (luts+ffs≈1500). angle_guard and frac_guard include negative/zero values to catch minimal-area feasible points; rounding is included because it can trade a little area for accuracy."
    },
    {
      "budget_share": 0.2,
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"choices": ["trunc", "round"], "high": null, "low": null, "param": "rounding"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 12, "param": "data_width"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 20, "low": 10, "param": "n_iter"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": -2, "param": "angle_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 2, "low": 0, "param": "frac_guard"},
        {"choices": null, "high": 4, "low": 2, "param": "k"}
      ],
      "why": "Unrolled_k replicates the datapath k times, so for the same accuracy it should be larger in area than iterative. The throughput constraint is only 1 MSPS and iterative already meets it, so unrolled_k is unlikely to offer a better area/accuracy trade-off. A small budget is reserved to confirm this and to catch any unexpected Fmax advantage that might allow a smaller N or W (e.g., if a narrower datapath becomes feasible at higher clock). k is limited to 2–4 because larger k only increases area without helping the area objective."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec minimises LUT+FF subject to a loose throughput floor (≥1 MSPS) and a modest accuracy floor (max_abs_err ≤ 2^-10). Iterative architectures naturally dominate this regime: they use one shared datapath, so their area is the smallest for any given (W, N, guards, rounding), and their serial schedule still delivers ≥4 MSPS for the largest N considered. The main search question is where the feasible accuracy boundary lies in (W, N, guard, rounding) space and how area grows along it. The proposed ranges bracket the likely feasible region (W 12–20, N 10–20) and include negative/zero guards to find minimal-area feasible corners, while extending high enough to map the Pareto front up to the HV reference area of 1500. Unrolled_k gets a small share only to verify that replication does not produce a surprise edge (e.g., via higher Fmax permitting smaller N) and to include any incidental Pareto points in the fleet merge. Round 1 uses broad ranges; later rounds will narrow around the discovered front and spend the reserved budget mapping it over the full feasible ranges."
}
